					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2000367&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607071824" alt="WrongJohnSilver" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2000367)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;" >WrongJohnSilver</a></span> <span class="odate time_1454019566 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jan 2016 22:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2447421">
									

<p>First, only if the effects were demonstrated to be strong enough in character to reach that end level. And second, whether or not the dissociation occurred, a widespread disbelief in cooperation would doom humanity in any case. Furthermore, we aren't really presented with evidence that the widespread disbelief is actually happening; it is merely presented as a hypothesis with some circumstantial evidence that we're supposed to just take at face value as happening.</p>
<p>On the contrary, the entire world has coordinated its efforts at a far greater and more aligned rate since the 1960s. For a world to be sliding back from a high-cooperation state would require it to take place in a much different world from the one we're in.</p>
<p>So, yes, the scenario you describe does result in extinction. But I can't read that scenario (or even a risk of that scenario) as existing based on my reading of the document.</p>

							</div>

								
